# Terraform Module Conventions

## Required layout

```
tf-modules/tf-{service}/
├── main.tf           # Resource definitions
├── variables.tf      # Input variables
├── outputs.tf        # Output values
├── providers.tf      # required_version + required_providers (when pinning)
└── README.md         # Description, Usage, Requirements, Inputs, Outputs
```

## Variables

- Name in `snake_case`.
- Always include `description` and `type`.
- Provide `default` for optional inputs; mark required inputs by omitting default and documenting in README.
- Group related variables together; avoid alphabetical scrambling.

## Outputs

- Name in `snake_case`.
- Include `description`.
- Expose stable identifiers (ARNs, IDs, DNS names) that downstream stacks need; do not expose internal implementation detail.

## Providers and versions

- Pin `required_version` in `providers.tf` (e.g. `~> 1.9.8`). The Terragrunt `before_hook` reads this value via `tfenv`.
- Pin `required_providers` versions when adding new providers.
- Do not set provider blocks (region, profile) inside modules; the root `terragrunt.hcl` injects those.

## No secrets, no environment values

- Modules are reusable across landscapes and environments. Do not hardcode landscape, env, account ID, or app alias inside a module.
- Pass those in via inputs.

## README.md sections

`## Description`, `## Usage`, `## Requirements`, `## Inputs`, `## Outputs`. Use a Markdown table for inputs and outputs with at least Name, Description, Type, Required (for inputs) and Description (for outputs).

## When adding a new module

- Mirror an existing similar module's structure (e.g. `tf-ssm_param`, `tf-ecs-service`).
- Add a brief stub README before merging; full content can iterate.
- Avoid breaking existing callers: add new inputs with safe defaults rather than renaming.
